nd said that I would fix it myself. One Saturday afternoon I hauled the machine into the yard and took a close look at it. As far as I could see, it needed only a minor adjustment: a turn of a screw here, a little tightening there, a drop of oil, and it would be as good as new. Inevitably the repair job was not quite so simple. The mower firmly refused to mow, so I decided to take it apart. The yard was soon littered with chunks of metal that had once made up a lawn mower. But I was extremely pleased with myself, for I had traced the cause of the trouble. One of the links in the chain that drove the wheels had snapped. After buying a new chain, I was faced with the insurmountable task of putting the confusing jigsaw puzzle back together. I was not surprised to find that the machine still refused to work after I had reassembled it, for the simple reason that I was left with several oddly shaped bits of metal that did not seem to fit anywhere. I gave up in despair. The weeks passed and the grass grew. When my wife nagged me to do something about it, I told her that I would either have to buy a new mower or let the grass grow. Needless to say, our house is now surrounded by a jungle. Buried somewhere in the deep grass is a rusting lawn mower that I have promised to fix one day.","explanation_cn":"课文说驱动轮子的链条上有一节链环断了。✓ one of the links had snapped."}'::jsonb,8) ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,stage,seq) DO UPDATE SET qtype=EXCLUDED.qtype,payload=EXCLUDED.payload,grammar_point_id=EXCLUDED.grammar_point_id;
INSERT INTO public.american_questions (lesson_id,stage,grammar_point_id,qtype,payload,seq) VALUES ('am3_l46',10,NULL,'choice','{"stem":"What did the writer find after reassembling the mower?","options":["He had lost the new chain","The mower ran better than new","The blades were sharp again","He was left with several odd bits of metal that fit nowhere"],"answer_index":3,"audio":"So great is our passion for doing things ourselves that we are becoming less and less dependent on skilled labor. No one can plead ignorance of a subject any longer, for there are countless do-it-yourself books and videos. Armed with the right tools and materials, newlyweds gaily embark on the task of decorating their own homes. Men, in particular, spend hours of their leisure time installing their own light fixtures, laying out their own gardens, building sheds, and making furniture. Some really keen enthusiasts go so far as to build their own computers. Stores cater for the do-it-yourself craze not only by running special advice desks for novices, but by offering customers parts they can assemble at home. Such things provide an excellent outlet for pent-up creative energy, but unfortunately not all of us are born handymen. Some wives tend to believe that their husbands are endlessly resourceful and can fix anything. Even men who can hardly drive a nail in straight are supposed to be born electricians, carpenters, plumbers, and mechanics. When a fuse blows, furniture gets rickety, pipes get clogged, or the dishwasher fails to run, some women assume that their husbands will somehow put things right. The worst thing about the do-it-yourself game is that sometimes even men live under the delusion that they can do anything, even when they have repeatedly been proved wrong. It is a matter of pride as much as anything else. Last spring my wife suggested that I call in a repairman to look at our lawn mower. It had broken down the previous summer, and though I had promised to fix it, I had never gotten round to it. I would not hear of the suggestion and said that I would fix it myself. One Saturday afternoon I hauled the machine into the yard and took a close look at it. As far as I could see, it needed only a minor adjustment: a turn of a screw here, a little tightening there, a drop of oil, and it would be as good as new. Inevitably the repair job was not quite so simple. The mower firmly refused to mow, so I decided to take it apart. The yard was soon littered with chunks of metal that had once made up a lawn mower. But I was extremely pleased with myself, for I had traced the cause of the trouble. One of the links in the chain that drove the wheels had snapped. After buying a new chain, I was faced with the insurmountable task of putting the confusing jigsaw puzzle back together. I was not surprised to find that the machine still refused to work after I had reassembled it, for the simple reason that I was left with several oddly shaped bits of metal that did not seem to fit anywhere. I gave up in despair. The weeks passed and the grass grew. When my wife nagged me to do something about it, I told her that I would either have to buy a new mower or let the grass grow. Needless to say, our house is now surrounded by a jungle. Buried somewhere in the deep grass is a rusting lawn mower that I have promised to fix one day.","explanation_cn":"课文说重新组装后,他手里还剩下几块形状古怪、哪都装不上的金属。✓ oddly shaped bits of metal."}'::jsonb,9) ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,stage,seq) DO UPDATE SET qtype=EXCLUDED.qtype,payload=EXCLUDED.payload,grammar_point_id=EXCLUDED.grammar_point_id;
INSERT INTO public.american_questions (lesson_id,stage,grammar_point_id,qtype,payload,seq) VALUES ('am3_l46',10,NULL,'choice','{"stem":"What did the writer finally tell his wife?","options":["He would either buy a new mower or let the grass grow","He would sell the house","He had fixed it perfectly","He would call an expert at once"],"answer_index":0,"audio":"So great is our passion for doing things ourselves that we are becoming less and less dependent on skilled labor. No one can plead ignorance of a subject any longer, for there are countless do-it-yourself books and videos. Armed with the right tools and materials, newlyweds gaily embark on the task of decorating their own homes. Men, in particular, spend hours of their leisure time installing their own light fixtures, laying out their own gardens, building sheds, and making furniture. Some really keen enthusiasts go so far as to build their own computers. Stores cater for the do-it-yourself craze not only by running special advice desks for novices, but by offering customers parts they can assemble at home. Such things provide an excellent outlet for pent-up creative energy, but unfortunately not all of us are born handymen. Some wives tend to believe that their husbands are endlessly resourceful and can fix anything. Even men who can hardly drive a nail in straight are supposed to be born electricians, carpenters, plumbers, and mechanics. When a fuse blows, furniture gets rickety, pipes get clogged, or the dishwasher fails to run, some women assume that their husbands will somehow put things right. The worst thing about the do-it-yourself game is that sometimes even men live under the delusion that they can do anything, even when they have repeatedly been proved wrong. It is a matter of pride as much as anything else. Last spring my wife suggested that I call in a repairman to look at our lawn mower. It had broken down the previous summer, and though I had promised to fix it, I had never gotten round to it. I would not hear of the suggestion and said that I would fix it myself. One Saturday afternoon I hauled the machine into the yard and took a close look at it. As far as I could see, it needed only a minor adjustment: a turn of a screw here, a little tightening there, a drop of oil, and it would be as good as new. Inevitably the repair job was not quite so simple. The mower firmly refused to mow, so I decided to take it apart. The yard was soon littered with chunks of metal that had once made up a lawn mower. But I was extremely pleased with myself, for I had traced the cause of the trouble. One of the links in the chain that drove the wheels had snapped. After buying a new chain, I was faced with the insurmountable task of putting the confusing jigsaw puzzle back together. I was not surprised to find that the machine still refused to work after I had reassembled it, for the simple reason that I was left with several oddly shaped bits of metal that did not seem to fit anywhere. I gave up in despair. The weeks passed and the grass grew. When my wife nagged me to do something about it, I told her that I would either have to buy a new mower or let the grass grow. Needless to say, our house is now surrounded by a jungle. Buried somewhere in the deep grass is a rusting lawn mower that I have promised to fix one day.","explanation_cn":"课文说妻子唠叨时,他说要么买新割草机,要么就让草长着。✓ buy a new mower or let the grass grow."}'::jsonb,10) ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,stage,seq) DO UPDATE SET qtype=EXCLUDED.qtype,payload=EXCLUDED.payload,grammar_point_id=EXCLUDED.grammar_point_id;
INSERT INTO public.american_lessons (id,book_no,unit_no,lesson_no,title_en,title_cn,grammar_focus,scene,prelisten_question,grammar_card) VALUES ('am3_l47',3,10,47,'Too High a Price?','代价太高?','the choice of A or B / 省略关系词的定语从句(things you can do)、现在分词修饰名词(dogs barking incessantly)、最高级 the highest / even more + 比较级、被动多形式(be found to do / It was revealed that / be kept awake by)、whatever 引导让步 + pay for / against these figures(原创承载 NCE3 第三册 L47 考点·无需净化:污染题材科普——垃圾、食品与噪音污染,幽默揭出最古老的噪音源竟是打鼾;本课为轻松科普/幽默,无暴力,正常试读;美式化 garbage/cell phone/neighbor)','污染,就是我们为这个人口过密、过度工业化的星球付出的代价。当你静下来想一想,处理垃圾其实只有四种办法:倾倒、焚烧、变成可再利用的东西,或者设法少产生一些。这四种办法我们一直都在试,可全世界产生的垃圾,单是那庞大的数量就有把我们淹没的危险。然而,垃圾只是污染这个星球的一部分问题。对越来越多廉价食物的需求,导致了另一种污染。工业化的农作方式生产出廉价的肉类,而大量使用杀虫剂和化肥又生产出廉价的谷物和蔬菜。我们为廉价食物付出的代价,也许已经太高了。就算你打算戒掉肉类、改吃素,也只能二选一:要么选价格昂贵的有机蔬菜,要么每次以为自己在吃新鲜色拉时,其实都在不停地摄入杀虫剂。不过,还有一种更为隐蔽的污染,尤其侵扰城市、闯入我们的日常生活,那就是噪音。半夜响起的汽车警报,除了惹恼邻居别无用处。警笛和风镐在街上朝我们尖叫,是极度烦躁的来源。一项关于噪音影响的调查显示——也许出人意料——夜里不停吠叫的狗,在一到七的评分上被列为最严重的噪音污染。调查还列出了一长串人们真正讨厌的声音:夏日午后嗡嗡作响的割草机、公寓楼里的深夜派对、吵闹的邻居、轰隆隆穿过安静小镇的大集装箱卡车、头顶飞过的飞机和直升机,还有公共场所里开到最大音量的音乐。新技术也为噪音添了一份“贡献”。很多人反感手机,尤其是在餐馆或公共交通上使用时——大声的通话闯进我们的思绪,打断了和朋友安静聊天的乐趣。这项调查还揭示了一个相当出人意料、甚至有点好笑的老式噪音源。原来是打鼾!人们发现,男人是最厉害的“冒犯者”:据披露,三十五岁上下的男人有百分之二十打鼾,到了六十多岁,这个数字更是飙升到令人惊愕的百分之六十。与这些数字相比,只有百分之五的女性经常打鼾,其余的则不断被身边吹号般的伴侣吵醒、或搅得无法入睡。无论噪音来自何处,有一点是肯定的:寂静,似乎已成了一段珍贵的回忆。','{"q":"Listen and answer — What does the writer describe as an ''amusing old-fashioned source of noise''?","options":["Snoring","Church bells","Steam engines","Street singers"],"answer_index":0}'::jsonb,'{"title":"the choice of A or B / 现在分词修饰名词 / 最高级·even more / 被动多形式 / whatever 让步","intro":"①the choice of A or B:在 A 和 B 之间选择;things (that) you can do 里关系词作宾语可省略。②现在分词短语放名词后修饰它,表主动:dogs barking incessantly。③the + 最高级表“最…”;even/still + more + 形容词表“更加…”。④被动多形式:be found to do(被发现…)、It was revealed that …(据披露…)、be kept awake by …(被…吵得睡不着)。⑤whatever + 名词引导让步:Whatever the source of noise, …;pay for(为…付出代价)、against these figures(与这些数字相比)。","table":{"headers":["用法","意思","例子"],"rows":[["the choice of A or B","在 A、B 间选择","the choice of organic food or cheap food"],["现在分词修饰名词","…着的…","dogs barking incessantly"],["even more + 形容词 / the 最高级","更加… / 最…","an even more insidious kind"],["be found to do / It was revealed that","被发现… / 据披露…","Men were found to be the worst"]]},"specialRules":[{"rule":"the choice of A or B 用 or 连接两项,不用 nor;of 不能换成 for。","mark":"choice of A or B"},{"rule":"whatever + 名词作让步状语(Whatever the source, …),不同于 however(接形容词/副词)、whenever(无论何时)。","mark":"Whatever the source"},{"rule":"美式:garbage/trash(英式 rubbish)、cell phone(英式 mobile)、neighbor(英式 neighbour)。本课已按美语写。","mark":"garbage / cell phone"}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET book_no=EXCLUDED.book_no,title_en=EXCLUDED.title_en,title_cn=EXCLUDED.title_cn,grammar_focus=EXCLUDED.grammar_focus,scene=EXCLUDED.scene,prelisten_question=EXCLUDED.prelisten_question,grammar_card=EXCLUDED.grammar_card;
INSERT INTO public.american_sentences (lesson_id,seq,speaker,text_en,text_cn) VALUES ('am3_l47',1,NULL,'Pollution is the price we pay for an overpopulated, over-industrialized planet. When you stop to think about it, there are only four things you can do with garbage: dump it, burn it, turn it into something you can use again, or try to produce less of it. We keep trying all four methods, but the sheer volume of garbage we produce worldwide threatens to overwhelm us.','污染,就是我们为这个人口过密、过度工业化的星球付出的代价。当你静下来想一想,处理垃圾其实只有四种办法:倾倒、焚烧、变成可再利用的东西,或者设法少产生一些。这四种办法我们一直都在试,可全世界产生的垃圾,单是那庞大的数量就有把我们淹没的危险。') ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,seq) DO UPDATE SET speaker=EXCLUDED.speaker,text_en=EXCLUDED.text_en,text_cn=EXCLUDED.text_cn;
INSERT INTO public.american_sentences (lesson_id,seq,speaker,text_en,text_cn) VALUES ('am3_l47',2,NULL,'Garbage, however, is only part of the problem of polluting our planet. The need to produce ever-larger quantities of cheap food leads to a different kind of pollution. Industrialized farming methods produce cheap meat, and the heavy use of pesticides and fertilizers produces cheap grain and vegetables. The price we pay for cheap food may already be too high. And if you think you will give up meat and become a vegetarian, you have the choice of either very expensive organically-grown vegetables or a steady diet of pesticides every time you eat what looks like a fresh salad.','然而,垃圾只是污染这个星球的一部分问题。对越来越多廉价食物的需求,导致了另一种污染。工业化的农作方式生产出廉价的肉类,而大量使用杀虫剂和化肥又生产出廉价的谷物和蔬菜。我们为廉价食物付出的代价,也许已经太高了。就算你打算戒掉肉类、改吃素,也只能二选一:要么选价格昂贵的有机蔬菜,要么每次以为自己在吃新鲜色拉时,其实都在不停地摄入杀虫剂。') ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,seq) DO UPDATE SET speaker=EXCLUDED.speaker,text_en=EXCLUDED.text_en,text_cn=EXCLUDED.text_cn;
INSERT INTO public.american_sentences (lesson_id,seq,speaker,text_en,text_cn) VALUES ('am3_l47',3,NULL,'However, there is an even more insidious kind of pollution that especially affects urban areas and invades our daily lives, and that is noise. Car alarms going off in the middle of the night serve only to annoy the neighbors. Sirens and jackhammers scream at us in the street and are a source of profound irritation. A recent survey of the effects of noise revealed, perhaps surprisingly, that dogs barking incessantly at night rated the highest form of noise pollution on a scale ranging from one to seven.','不过,还有一种更为隐蔽的污染,尤其侵扰城市、闯入我们的日常生活,那就是噪音。半夜响起的汽车警报,除了惹恼邻居别无用处。警笛和风镐在街上朝我们尖叫,是极度烦躁的来源。一项关于噪音影响的调查显示——也许出人意料——夜里不停吠叫的狗,在一到七的评分上被列为最严重的噪音污染。') ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,seq) DO UPDATE SET speaker=EXCLUDED.speaker,text_en=EXCLUDED.text_en,text_cn=EXCLUDED.text_cn;
INSERT INTO public.american_sentences (lesson_id,seq,speaker,text_en,text_cn) VALUES ('am3_l47',4,NULL,'The survey turned up a long list of sounds that people really dislike: lawn mowers whining on a summer afternoon, late-night parties in apartment buildings, noisy neighbors, big container trucks thundering through quiet towns, planes and helicopters flying overhead, and loud music played at maximum volume in public places. New technology has made its own contribution to noise, too. Many people object to cell phones, especially when they are used in restaurants or on public transportation, where loud conversations invade our thoughts and interrupt the pleasure of a quiet chat with friends.','调查还列出了一长串人们真正讨厌的声音:夏日午后嗡嗡作响的割草机、公寓楼里的深夜派对、吵闹的邻居、轰隆隆穿过安静小镇的大集装箱卡车、头顶飞过的飞机和直升机,还有公共场所里开到最大音量的音乐。新技术也为噪音添了一份贡献。很多人反感手机,尤其是在餐馆或公共交通上使用时——大声的通话闯进我们的思绪,打断了和朋友安静聊天的乐趣。') ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,seq) DO UPDATE SET speaker=EXCLUDED.speaker,text_en=EXCLUDED.text_en,text_cn=EXCLUDED.text_cn;
INSERT INTO public.american_sentences (lesson_id,seq,speaker,text_en,text_cn) VALUES ('am3_l47',5,NULL,'The survey also revealed a rather surprising and possibly amusing old-fashioned source of noise. It turned out to be snoring! Men were found to be the worst offenders. It was revealed that twenty percent of men in their mid-thirties snore, and that this figure rises to a staggering sixty percent of men in their sixties. Against these figures, it was found that only five percent of women snore regularly, while the rest are constantly woken or kept awake by their trumpeting partners. Whatever the source of noise, one thing is certain: silence, it seems, has become a golden memory.','这项调查还揭示了一个相当出人意料、甚至有点好笑的老式噪音源。原来是打鼾!人们发现,男人是最厉害的“冒犯者”:据披露,三十五岁上下的男人有百分之二十打鼾,到了六十多岁,这个数字更是飙升到令人惊愕的百分之六十。与这些数字相比,只有百分之五的女性经常打鼾,其余的则不断被身边吹号般的伴侣吵醒、或搅得无法入睡。无论噪音来自何处,有一点是肯定的:寂静,似乎已成了一段珍贵的回忆。') ON CONFLICT (lesson_id,seq) DO UPDATE SET speaker=EXCLUDED.speaker,text_en=EXCLUDED.text_en,text_cn=EXCLUDED.text_cn;
